[38;5;231m⏺[39m Good — [38;5;153m--out PATH[39m locked in.

  Next: [1mwhat the payloads actually contain.[0m This is a stored format that downstream consumers will parse, so it's the
  least reversible choice in the whole feature.

  [38;5;153mstore.daily_rows()[39m hands back [38;5;153m(account_id, cents)[39m with cents as an integer straight from the [38;5;153mbilling_daily.cents[39m
  column; [38;5;153mrender_text()[39m divides by 100 only at display time.

  - [1mInteger cents[0m — [38;5;153m{"account_id": "acct-1", "cents": 1050}[39m / [38;5;153macct-1,1050[39m. Lossless, identical to the database column,
    no rounding to reason about, and re-aggregating exported files stays exact. Consumers divide by 100 themselves.
  - [1mDecimal-string dollars[0m — [38;5;153m"10.50"[39m. Matches what ops already sees in the cron email, so a human comparing file to
    email sees the same characters. Every consumer has to parse a decimal, and anyone who reaches for a float
    re-introduces the rounding you avoided.
  - [1mFloat dollars[0m — [38;5;153m10.5[39m. Easiest to consume naively, and the one option with a real correctness cost: binary floats on
    money drift under summation, which matters because these are billing totals.

  [1mMy recommendation: integer cents.[0m It's the representation the database and [38;5;153mdaily_rows()[39m already use, so [38;5;153mexport[39m stays a
  faithful dump rather than a lossy transform, and the dollars formatting stays where it belongs — in the human-facing
  text renderer. This is the choice you can't cheaply revisit: once a nightly file lands in someone's pipeline, changing
  the unit silently breaks them by a factor of 100.
